  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The position $x$ of a particle at time $t$ is given by $x(t)=2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({t}^{3}+{e}^{t}).$ Calculate the velocity at time $t.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "The velocity is the derivative of the position function $x(t)$ with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}\u2061({t}^{3}+{e}^{t})) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({t}^{3}+{e}^{t})\\cdot (3{t}^{2}+{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =-2(3{t}^{2}+{e}^{t})\\operatorname{sin}\u2061({t}^{3}+{e}^{t})\\end{aligned}\n$$",
